   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "One might object that the Liar says that it itself is false, not say that some other (existent or non-existent) declarative is false. This brings things back to al-Samarqandī’s “as if” claim. While he says that it is “as if” the speaker who utters the Liar utters two declaratives, his actual view appears to be that the speaker really does utter two declaratives—or, at least, really does need to utter two declaratives if they wish to avoid a vacuous subject. In support of this, he says that the p",
   "md_text": "One might object that the Liar says that it itself is false, not say that some other (existent or non-existent) declarative is false. This brings things back to al-Samarqandī’s “as if” claim. While he says that it is “as if” the speaker who utters the Liar utters two declaratives, his actual view appears to be that the speaker really does utter two declaratives—or, at least, really does need to utter two declaratives if they wish to avoid a vacuous subject. In support of this, he says that the p"
  },
  {
   "i": 83,
   "shape_h": "ol",
   "shape_m": "ol",
   "html_text": "The Liar is false. (Assumption) If so, it is not both true and false. (From (1), by the principle defended above) If so, it is not false (A fallacious inference from (2)) If so, it is true. If so, it is both true and false.",
   "md_text": "The Liar is false. (Assumption) If so, it is not both true and false. (From (1), by the principle defended above) If so, it is not false (A fallacious inference from (2)) If so, it is true. If so, it is both true and false."
  }
